# Git Workflow

## Never push or deploy without explicit approval

Pushing, force-pushing, opening PRs, deploying, and anything else that leaves
the local machine requires the user to say go ahead first. Committing locally
is fine and does not need approval.

When work is ready, stop and ask. Report the branch name and what is on it,
then wait.

```bash
# ❌ BAD — publishing without being asked
git commit -m "..." && git push

# ✅ GOOD — commit locally, then stop and ask
git commit -m "..."
# "Committed to feature/contact-rate-limit. Want me to push it?"
```

## Work on a branch, never directly on main

Before making changes, branch off up-to-date `main`. Follow the existing
`feature/<short-description>` convention, or `fix/` and `chore/` when that
describes the work better.

```bash
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout -b feature/contact-rate-limit
```

If changes have already been started on `main` by mistake, move them onto a
branch before committing:

```bash
git checkout -b feature/contact-rate-limit  # carries uncommitted work across
```

Keep `main` clean so it always matches what is deployed.
